{"name":"Aloe Nectar","hex":"#dfe2c9","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aloe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The resins of the tree Aquilaria malaccensis (syn. Aquilaria agallocha), known for their fragrant aroma, produced after infection by the fungus Phialophora parasitica.","Any plant of the large and variable genus Aloe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aloe"},{"term":"nectar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The drink of the gods. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any delicious drink, now especially a type of sweetened fruit juice."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To feed on nectar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nectar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aloe","blocks":[{"text":"Aloe is a genus containing 594 species of flowering succulent plants. The most widely known species is Aloe vera, or \"true aloe\", which is cultivated as a source for topical uses and dietary supplements. Other species, such as Aloe ferox, are also cultivated or harvested from the wild for similar applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg/330px-Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aloe","wikidata":"Q127134"},{"title":"Nectar","blocks":[{"text":"Nectar is a viscous, sugar-rich liquid produced by glands in plants called nectaries, either within the flowers with which it attracts pollinating animals, or by extrafloral nectaries, which provide a nutrient source to animal mutualists, which in turn provide herbivore protection. Common nectar-consuming pollinators include mosquitoes, hoverflies, wasps, bees, butterflies and moths, hummingbirds, honeyeaters and bats. \nNectar is an economically important substance as it is the sugar source for honey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Nectar.jpg/330px-Nectar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nectar","wikidata":"Q171934"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg/500px-Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg/500px-Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_(4387600468).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/500px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/1280px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/500px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Succulent plants, such as aloe, store water in their enlarged fleshy leaves","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASplit_Aloe.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg/500px-Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg/1280px-Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg/500px-Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped